
Done. Below is the integration, the four banks, and the worked example. One reversal from what I put to you earlier is flagged in §1 — it's the only place I've decided against something I previously offered, and it needs your sign-off.

## 0. Locality note

You've asked that nothing here depend on the other reviews. Two arguments I made earlier were phrased in terms of specific downstream engines. Restated locally:

- **Why inline provenance annotations decay.** Any consumer that reads the contract and emits its own artefact rather than passing the contract through will not necessarily carry inline marks forward. After two such hops, `[default]` or `[assumed]` is gone and only the value remains. This is a property of chained re-emission, not of any particular consumer.
- **Why a missing value is safer than a wrong one.** A consumer that encounters an absent value must either ask or halt. A consumer that encounters a populated value proceeds. The asymmetry holds for any consumer that doesn't independently re-derive the field.

Neither argument needs the other engines to stand. Where the spec text below says "downstream consumer," it means *any* reader of the contract, including a human.

## 1. Uncertainty rendering — consolidated, with one reversal

```
D.11  RENDERING OF UNRESOLVED SLOTS

  none stated        Input addressed nothing here and nothing further
                     is expected. Terminal. Requirement slots only.

  not established    Remains open after discovery. Definition slots
                     only. MUST also appear in open:.

  [N/A] — <reason>   Inapplicable to this goal. Reason required,
                     one clause. Any slot.

  open: <slots>      Closing line. Lists every slot rendered
                     "not established", comma-separated, in contract
                     order. Omitted entirely if none.

  A slot is never omitted from the contract. An absent field and an
  unconsidered field are indistinguishable to a downstream reader.
```

**The reversal.** I earlier asked you to confirm that `objective: none stated` would be the new render for a missing end state. I've decided against it and made objective a definition slot, so a missing objective renders `not established` and lands in `open:`.

Reason: `none stated` is terminal — it tells a downstream reader that nothing further is expected. That is true of an absent invalidation condition and false of an absent objective. A contract with no end state is not a goal statement; it's a topic. Letting it render smoothly was the one place where the softening I recommended would have cost real information rather than just noise.

If you'd rather keep objective terminal-renderable, say so and I'll flip it back, but I'd be recording a dissent.

**The partition is now load-bearing.** `open:` is bounded only because definition slots are a closed set of four. That means the spec has to *enumerate* them rather than leave the class inferable. As they've surfaced in this review:

| Definition slots (may enter `open:`) | Requirement slots (never) |
|---|---|
| objective, audience, environment, form | exclusions, invalidation, success, horizon, register, … |

If the live spec names these differently or has a fifth definition slot, the banks below map by dimension rather than by name, and the `open:` bound becomes five. This is the one item on the remaining list I'd still call architecture, not calibration.

## 2. G.3a with example banks

```
G.3a  TYPE 2 SCAFFOLDING

  A Type 2 question MAY carry illustrative examples drawn from the
  fixed bank for that slot. Examples are NEVER generated per case.

  Examples communicate answer shape, not answer content. They are
  not options, defaults, recommendations, or categories. An answer
  is user-derived whether or not it matches an example.

  Bank constraints: 3–5 entries · one abstraction level above the
  expected answer · spanning the dimension rather than clustering ·
  at least one entry not domain-adjacent to typical inputs ·
  alphabetical order, carrying no ranking.

  Render: inline, dot-separated, single line. Not bulleted — a
  bulleted list reads as a menu.

  Skipping sends the slot to open:. It never populates a value.

  Header, once per round:
    "Anything you skip will be left open rather than guessed."
```

**The four banks.**

```
objective — "What should be true when this is done?"
  a decision is made · a document exists · a process runs without
  you · someone's behaviour changes

audience — "Who is this for?"
  businesses · internal employees · retail investors · students

environment — "Where does this operate?"
  a regulated industry · a school · a team with no technical staff ·
  an offline setting · an open-source project

form — "What form should the result take?"
  a checklist · a conversation · a piece of software · a training
  session · a written document
```

Each is alphabetical, generic enough that no entry could be pasted into a contract and read as complete, and spanning: objective covers decision / artefact / system / behaviour; environment covers regulatory / institutional / capability / infrastructural / social.

Note the cost the audience bank pays in the example below. `retail investors` is a generic entry that happens to coincide with the true answer for financial-services inputs. A fixed bank will occasionally do this, and when it does we cannot distinguish discovery from echo. Per policy the answer is user-derived regardless. This is the priced-in cost of fixing the bank rather than generating it, and it's the right side of the trade — a generated bank would coincide *deliberately* and constantly.

## 3. Worked example

**Input:** *"I want to build an AI financial advisor. It has to be FCA compliant. I don't want it giving specific product recommendations."*

D.9 determines under a single reading: environment (FCA compliance names a regulatory regime), form ("build" plus "AI" names software), exclusions (stated directly). Objective is *not* determined — the input names an artefact, not an end state. Audience is not determined.

**Old rendering:**

```
objective:    [UNSPECIFIED]
audience:     [UNSPECIFIED]
environment:  UK retail financial services (FCA-regulated)
form:         software product
exclusions:   no specific product recommendations
invalidation: [UNSPECIFIED]
success:      [UNSPECIFIED]
horizon:      [UNSPECIFIED]
register:     [UNSPECIFIED]
```

Six tokens, uniform weight. The two that will change what gets built are indistinguishable from the four that won't.

**New rendering:**

```
objective:    not established
audience:     not established
environment:  UK retail financial services (FCA-regulated)
form:         software product
exclusions:   no specific product recommendations
invalidation: none stated
success:      none stated
horizon:      none stated
register:     [N/A] — form is software; register is fixed at
              interface design, not at goal level

open: objective, audience
```

Same information. `none stated` reads as a normal sentence about the input rather than as an alarm; the two live gaps are named once, in one place, where a consumer looks rather than scans.

**Type 2 round as rendered to the user:**

```
Anything you skip will be left open rather than guessed.

1. What should be true when this is done?
   (Describe the end state, not the thing being built.)

   Examples of the kind of answer: a decision is made · a document
   exists · a process runs without you · someone's behaviour changes

   Describe it in your own words, or say "skip".

2. Who is this for?
   (Describe the intended audience.)

   Examples of the kind of answer: businesses · internal employees ·
   retail investors · students

   Describe another audience in your own words, or say "skip".
```

The parenthetical on Q1 is doing the elicitation work you identified — a user who typed "an AI financial advisor" has named an artefact and will tend to re-name it. Naming the *category error* is what unblocks them, and it's something a bank of examples can't do on its own.

**After a partial answer** — user answers 2 (`retail investors — people with savings but no adviser`), skips 1:

```
objective:    not established
audience:     retail investors — people with savings but no adviser
...
open: objective
```

Audience is user-derived and unmarked; the verbatim match to a bank entry earns no annotation, per G.3a. Objective stays open. Nothing invented, and a downstream consumer has one unambiguous instruction: the end state is unresolved and must be asked before anything is optimised against it.

## 4. Delta against what you approved

Adopted as agreed: differentiated soft forms; bounded `open:`; no adoption on silence; skips to `open:` never to values; fixed banks; examples as shape not content; inline dot-separated render.

Changed: objective reclassified as a definition slot, so it renders `not established` rather than `none stated` — reversing what I offered earlier (§1).

Added: explicit enumeration of the definition/requirement partition, since `open:`'s bound now depends on it.

## 5. Remaining items

Calibration, as you said, with two exceptions worth naming:

- **Echo rate is not enforceable in-prompt.** The >40% threshold is an out-of-band review commitment — someone has to sample answers against banks periodically. If that won't happen, the banks are unmonitored and the "at least one out-of-domain entry" rule is the only live guard. Better to know that now than to have a metric in the spec that nobody owns.
- **The `[N/A]` reason clause is unbounded.** One clause is the instruction; nothing enforces it. If reasons start growing into paragraphs, `[N/A]` becomes the attention magnet that `[UNSPECIFIED]` was. Worth a length cap if you want it airtight.

Want me to produce the full consolidated spec text next, or is the delta enough to fold in on your side?
